            <ArticleTitle>The efficacy of transdermal fentanyl for pain relief after endoscopic submucosal dissection: a prospective, randomised controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>925-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.dld.2012.06.015</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1590-8658(12)00237-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Epigastric pain management following endoscopic submucosal dissection is an important consideration. This study aimed to investigate the utility and safety of fentanyl patches for pain relief after the procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients who were scheduled to undergo endoscopic submucosal dissection were prospectively randomised to either a transdermal fentanyl patch group or a placebo control group. An additional pethidine was intravenously administered when pain developed and a numerical rating scale was used to evaluate both pre- and post-procedural pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One hundred and ten patients were randomly assigned to receive either a 12 mcg/h fentanyl patch or a control patch on the night before the procedure. The fentanyl patch group had significantly lower pain scores immediately following the procedure (mean, 5.17 vs. 4.26, p=0.030). Maximal pain scores during the first 24 h (5.43 vs. 4.46, p=0.038) and pain scores on the day after the procedure (2.98 vs. 1.20, p&lt;0.001) were also lower in the fentanyl patch group. In addition, the fentanyl patch group required a significantly lower dose of pethidine for pain management (24.54 vs. 11.25, p=0.004).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The application of a transdermal fentanyl patch is an effective, convenient, and safe method to control epigastric pain after endoscopic submucosal dissection.</AbstractText>
